#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "0bc42213-4681-59fb-9390-e56740cf1f1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5784" :question/problem "Factor $m^2 + 16n^2$." :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16n^{2} &= m^{2} - (-16n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (16\\textrm{i}^{2}n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (4^{2}\\textrm{i}^{2}n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (4\\textrm{i}n)^{2} \\\\\n&= (m + 4\\textrm{i}n)(m - 4\\textrm{i}n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "6b17455c-780d-5843-b9cb-3d1a8abdb4ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5785" :question/problem "Factor $25x^4 + 4 y^8$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n25x^4 + 4 y^8 = \\left(5x^2\\right)^2 + \\left(2 y^4\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{4} + 4y^{8} &= 25x^{4} - (-4y^{8}) \\\\\n&= 25x^{4} - (4\\textrm{i}^{2}y^{8}) \\\\\n&= (5x^{2})^{2} - (2\\textrm{i}y^{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= (5x^{2} + 2\\textrm{i}y^{4})(5x^{2} - 2\\textrm{i}y^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "7d37eaaf-02aa-5a0b-b0a3-32b3c5b7ec35" :question/math-academy-id "e-5786" :question/problem "Factor $-z^3-25z$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor $-z$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-z^3-25z =-z(z^2+25)\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(z^2+25\\right)\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 25 &= z^{2} - (-25) \\\\\n&= z^{2} - (25\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= z^{2} - (5\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= (z + 5\\textrm{i})(z - 5\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n-z(z^2+25)=-z(z+5\\textrm i)(z-5\\textrm i)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "2df8c4e2-04ec-5b14-904f-cbc8dcf1d35e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5787" :question/problem "Factor $x^4+18x^2+81$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in $x^2$. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} + 18x^{2} + 81 &= (x^{2})^{2} + 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot (x^{2}) + (9)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 9)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 9 &= x^{2} - (-9) \\\\\n&= x^{2} - (9\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= (x + 3\\textrm{i})(x - 3\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} + 18x^{2} + 81 &= (x^{2} + 9)^{2} \\\\\n&= ((x + 3\\textrm{i})(x - 3\\textrm{i}))^{2} \\\\\n&= (x + 3\\textrm{i})^{2}(x - 3\\textrm{i})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
